{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/session-11afc0ac-review.md", "content": "# Review: varied demo geometries need a shared-rule check

This instance supplied seven highly varied demonstration pairs before a 5x17 test: large uniform stripes with local marks, lines/seeds in different orientations, sparse markers, and outputs involving fills or extensions. The responses repeatedly requested examples and reread overlapping skill references without first extracting a usable per-demo delta ledger; ultimately the answer was submitted without a validated common mechanism. This is a process review, not a verified task solution.

For future heterogeneous demo streams, classify each pair's geometry and roles, then record only the exact changed regions and informative unchanged candidates. Track what happens to boundary stripes, isolated markers, seed colors, and intervening/background cells. Form a candidate operation or explicit conditional rule that explains every demo, including negative evidence; matching palette alone is not a bridge between unrelated-looking cases. Before another paid request, articulate test-prediction alternatives and a concrete feature that could discriminate them. Once the core workflow guidance is loaded, stop browsing duplicate references and spend the turn on evidence extraction or the next legal puzzle action.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/session-11afc0ac-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-discipline", "file": "references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous object-cleaning demos: session note

When several differently sized grids contain solid monochrome rectangles among sparse alien-colored marks, don't infer a simple global recoloring from palette overlap. Compare each pair's deltas and classify objects: the demos can show preservation of intact rectangles while isolated noise is erased and intrusions inside a target object are repaired. Record separately (a) isolated marks cleared to the background, (b) interior foreign-color cells replaced by the enclosing object's color, (c) genuine missing cells filled to complete a rectangle, and (d) non-target/background regions left unchanged. Do not automatically fill every connected component's bounding box: validate each proposed target's original geometry and its output mask. The common operation may be shape- or context-selected rather than applying to every colored cell.

After a wrong answer, reject the entire selection/repair hypothesis and replay all demos; changing only the set of speckles or rectangle bounds is not sufficient. A new paid example is worthwhile only when surviving selection rules predict different test outputs. Mechanically generate and validate the output grid before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-discipline/references/heterogeneous-shape-cleaning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-panel-overlay-inference", "file": "references/panel-overlay-session-review.md", "content": "# Panel-overlay session review

A stream used separator rows or columns to divide inputs into equal-sized aligned panels; outputs retained one panel's dimensions while reducing the palette. Multiple early projections failed because the candidate mask and output baseline were conflated. The successful test projection used the common panel color as the output baseline (0) and marked cells with 3 where aligned panel cells matched that color; cells not meeting the predicate remained baseline 0. The same broad match/baseline model accounted for demos with other shared colors and differing panel orientations.

Reusable lesson: infer geometry, cell predicate, and output encoding as separate questions. Verify each against every demo, including deviations and negative evidence. A plausible shared-color intersection from one example is insufficient; distinguish exact cell equality from foreground overlap, and determine whether output preserves one source panel or normalizes all cells to the common color. Before paying for demos or spending attempts, keep candidate full-grid projections and act only when evidence warrants one.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-panel-overlay-inference/references/panel-overlay-session-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-lessons", "file": "references/noisy-motif-projection.md", "content": "# Sparse motif projection: review lesson

A demo had a diagonal multi-color marker pattern, while the test contained a compact related motif. The response guessed a single local color adjustment without establishing the transformation. Generalizable lesson: derive the full rule from all changed and unchanged demo coordinates, then apply that operation to the entire test motif; do not preserve most input cells merely because they appear unrelated. Where the demo adds diagonal runs, enumerate the rays and their bounds explicitly and distinguish them from row/column continuation or marker replacement. This is a caution about the analysis method, not a confirmed rule for the particular puzzle.
