{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/marker-propagation-boundary-validation.md", "content": "# Marker propagation and boundary validation

In noisy ARC grids with one or more special-color seeds, do not assume the marker spreads through every cell that is not another color. A useful competing mechanism is flood-fill through only the designated background color, with other non-background colors acting as barriers. Simulate both candidate connectivity domains and compare the complete predicted footprint against all demo outputs, including untouched cells and grid-edge contact. Preserve original seeds and all cells outside the proven domain. Only project a propagation rule onto the test after reproducing each demo exactly; check shape, row widths, palette, and changed-cell count mechanically.

A propagation footprint that is surprisingly large is not itself evidence for unrestricted fill. Determine whether the allowed-color component is enclosed or connected to the boundary. If demonstrations fail to distinguish allowed-color connectivity from unrestricted flood fill, spend a paid demo only if those hypotheses generate materially different test outputs and a new example is likely to settle the distinction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/marker-propagation-boundary-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review", "file": "references/periodic-row-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic row-sequence demonstrations

When a demo contains a compact non-background motif and the test contains full-width rows of single colors, consider whether the task is sequence extrapolation across rows rather than a cell-local transformation. Read the input/output row-by-row, identify unchanged rows and inserted/recolored rows, and infer any cycle only after checking the complete output. Apply the resulting sequence to the test's row structure and verify every row, including the cycle boundary; do not assume original test rows are all preserved as markers.

For the reviewed example, the 5x5 input rows were `1,9,7,0,0`; `9,1,9,7,0`; `0,9,1,9,7`; `0,0,9,1,9`; `0,0,0,9,1`. The output continued/filled the row-wise periodic pattern so each row became a repeated sequence of colors, with row starts cycling `1,9,7,9,1`. This is a clue to test row-cycle extrapolation, not sufficient alone to assert a unique rule for a different test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-review/references/periodic-row-sequence.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-gap-span-evidence-review", "file": "references/session-c2bb8289-review.md", "content": "# Session review: sparse 9-marker grid with heterogeneous demos

This instance included demonstrations in several palettes and dimensions. Some showed inserted color-2 cells along aligned marker runs, while outputs also contained informative unchanged gaps and clusters. Multiple projected guesses based on connecting all consecutive aligned markers, filling only single-cell gaps, and related span variants were incorrect. The available evidence therefore did not validate a simple universal interpolation rule for the test pattern.

Reusable lesson: keep exact changed/unchanged coordinates for every demo; explain why each candidate gap changes or remains unchanged. After a wrong output, abandon the full rule, not merely its span parameters. A later demo may clarify some local behavior without proving how it maps to a structurally different test. This record documents unresolved ambiguity, not a successful solution.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-gap-span-evidence-review/references/session-c2bb8289-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reassessment.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demo stream: evidence and reassessment

This session contained seven demonstrations with markedly different dimensions and structures (stripes, isolated markers, directional lines, and sparse motifs), followed by a 5×17 test. Repeated demo requests and reference reads did not establish a common transformation. The submitted test guess recolored the 9-background and 6 bands by an unsupported extrapolation and was incorrect. No solved transformation was established.

Reusable process lesson: classify each pair's geometry and roles before seeking a common rule. Track exact changed-cell regions, retained markers, background/boundary colors, and informative unchanged areas. Palette overlap alone is not a bridge between geometries. A viable candidate must replay every pair, including negative evidence, and fully specify how it projects to the test. If candidates remain, compare their complete test grids and spend a demo only for a concrete discriminator that could change the decision. After a wrong answer, reject the full rule and rebuild from all pairs; do not tweak cells on the submitted output. This note documents process, not a task solution.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-reassessment.md"}
